ging the laws in order to provide  more tests for uh hgv drivers and  encouraging people to come back into the  into the market but you're right there  are longer term problems which is it's a  difficult uh a long day's work it's a  hard work it's a skilled job and  actually it's been underpaid up until  now so we very much welcome the salaries  going up the wages going up and that's  attracting more people back to the  sector a quick fix would be short-term  visa for drivers in europe to come to  the uk  i'll look at everything and we'll move  to heaven and earth to do whatever we  can to  make sure that shortages are alleviated  with hgv drivers but we need to look at  the things which are going to make a  difference and the big problem is that  the the testing of drivers coming in you  get an attrition rate people retire if  you can't get new people in and we we  were unable to test 40 000 drivers  during coronavirus that's the principal  um problem uh and of course there are  already six million eu nationals with  settled and pre-settled status some of  whom will already be eligible to to  drive  enticing them to come here is really a  higher salary  and we just need to make sure that the  tests can be passed if people have  perhaps have an expired license but want  to get back to driving a lorry  move heaven and earth to get them to  come here reports this morning in the  newspapers that the environment  secretary wants uh these visas for  skilled workers to um have a short-term  visa for lorry drivers from europe you  and the home secretary do not is there  any truth in that story  no actually we're one on this i think  he's actually talking about the seasonal  workers um schemes specifically and as i  said i wouldn't rule out doing something  or you know any of the measures that are  possible to take for lorry drivers my  point is that over a long period of time  what we've done in this country is rely  on importing cheap european often  eastern european labor undercutting the  domestic market and creating more  long-term problems that we end up never  actually getting out of this cycle which  is to say has existed for years i  welcome the fact that you know lorry  drivers are able to attract a better  salary and that's what we need including  better conditions as well and you know  99  of lorry drivers in this country are  white male average age 55  we we need to make this a more  attractive  industry to go into and and the solution  to that is to have those slightly higher  wages and better conditions at truck  stops and the like as well  um  these short-term visas these specialists  skilled worker visas ballerinas are on  the list and hgv drivers are not  well as i say  i wouldn't rule anything out here but  what i don't want to do is exactly but  that's absurd  yeah  but the difference here is that the real  problem that we face and we know this  because as soon as the tests become  available they are snapped up  immediately the real bottleneck in all  of this is testing that is where we need  to release it you've made the point as  far as that's concerned mr champs were  talking about bringing in drivers in the  interim to try and fix this problem  we're 110 000 drivers short there are  drivers 14 000 drivers went home during  the pandemic only 600 have come back by  any maths you know there's a good 10 000  even with slippage who could come back  and at least try to help in the short  term they can't get a skill worker uh  visa but a ballerina can  i should say those numbers are hotly  debated but i won't go into all of that  um what i would say is we've got six  million uh as i say eu settled and  pre-settled status many of those people  can already drive we've also got  hundreds of thousands of brits who are  able to drive and are being attracted  back so  we'll do whatever it takes as a setup  we'll move heaven and earth and i'll  look at any feasible scheme we've  already taken measures which have  doubled the number of tests available  including changing the law um so we're  using every lever uh at our disposal to  to do this uh you're gonna look at the  skill worker visa is that what you're  saying  absolutely not ruling anything out at  all we want to see this problem resolved  but i also want to see it resolve for  the long term not just a short term fix  which ends up exacerbating the long term  as well how many four courts were  affected this morning  well out of bp's uh 12 or 1300 for four  courts uh yesterday they told me that  five had to be closed throughout the  country and say there's no shortage of  vp that supplies fuel though  uh bp and um tesco the others morrisons  and uh asda and other supermarkets  saying they have no problems as have  other uh petrol companies so it has been  quite a defined problem this is not a  shortage of fuel at the refineries uh  and it's not something new in fact when  people don't matter if it is or not if  you can't get it from the refinery as  tony blair found out all those years ago  you can't get it from the refinery to  the forecourt it doesn't matter where  the problem is that was different  because they had massive numbers of uh  of hgv drivers not working there was an  actual strike there was a blockade it  was a very different situation but the  point is they couldn't get it from the  refinery to the forecourt that's a  similar going to be a similar problem if  we can't get more drivers my question to  you is transport secretary this morning  is how many four courts are affected  well as i say five yesterday on the bp  four courts the point i was going to  make though uh okay uh only five petrol  stations four courts in the whole  country that's impacted  of bp yesterday that's the case but okay  the point was going to be about the  others  let's clarify this point because you're  saying that there isn't an issue  all the front pages this morning are  saying that there is an issue the petrol  retailers association is 